-- Q65: Business Law: Contracts [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Acceptance was effective Thursday upon dispatch under the mailbox rule, forming a contract before the revocation arrived; if Baker had instead mailed a rejection Wednesday then acceptance Thursday, the first-to-arrive rule would control","No contract formed because the revocation was mailed before the acceptance, so the mailbox rule treats both communications identically","The contract formed Wednesday when Allen mailed the revocation, because revocations take priority over acceptances under common law","The contract formed Friday when Baker received the revocation, because all communications are effective only upon actual receipt"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the common law mailbox rule (Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 63), an acceptance is effective upon dispatch while a revocation is effective only upon receipt. Baker''s Thursday acceptance was effective when mailed, before Allen''s revocation arrived, forming a valid contract. If Baker had mailed a rejection first on Wednesday and then an acceptance on Thursday, the mailbox rule would not apply and whichever communication arrives first controls.
Wrong (B): The mailbox rule does not treat revocations and acceptances identically. Acceptances are effective on dispatch; revocations require receipt.
Wrong (C): Revocations do not take priority over acceptances. A revocation must be received before the acceptance is dispatched to prevent contract formation.
Wrong (D): Acceptances under the mailbox rule are effective upon dispatch, not upon receipt. Only revocations require actual receipt.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 65;

-- Q66: Agency and Regulation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Authority explicitly granted to the agent in a written or oral agreement by the principal","Authority a third party reasonably believes the agent has based on the principal''s prior conduct or representations","Authority the agent assumes on their own initiative without the principal''s knowledge or consent","Authority conferred upon the agent by operation of law or court order in emergency situations"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 2.03, apparent authority arises when a principal''s conduct causes a third party to reasonably believe the agent has authority. Because Crestwood never notified Hanover of Larson''s termination, Hanover reasonably relied on prior dealings, binding Crestwood through apparent authority.
Wrong (A): This describes actual express authority, not apparent authority. Larson''s express authority was revoked in January 2026.
Wrong (C): This describes inherent or unauthorized agency, not apparent authority. Apparent authority depends on the principal''s conduct toward the third party.
Wrong (D): Court-ordered authority is not the concept at issue here. Apparent authority arises from the principal''s representations, not judicial action.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 66;

-- Q67: Agency and Regulation [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Thornton is liable in both scenarios because employers are always vicariously liable for employee conduct regardless of the circumstances or location","Thornton is likely liable for the coffee detour as a minor deviation within scope, but not the 30-mile personal errand on a day off, which is a frolic outside scope; Mitchell is personally liable in both","Mitchell alone is liable in both scenarios because employers are never held vicariously liable for employee negligence under respondeat superior doctrine","Thornton is liable only for the personal errand because the delivery-route coffee stop was entirely Mitchell''s personal decision and sole responsibility"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 7.07, an employer is vicariously liable under respondeat superior for employee torts within the scope of employment. A 15-minute coffee detour is a minor deviation that keeps Mitchell within scope, so Thornton is liable. The 30-mile personal trip on a day off is a frolic outside scope, relieving Thornton. Mitchell remains personally liable in both because respondeat superior adds employer liability rather than replacing employee responsibility.
Wrong (A): Employers are not liable for all employee conduct. Liability requires the tort to occur within the scope of employment.
Wrong (C): Respondeat superior does impose vicarious liability on employers for employee torts committed within the scope of employment.
Wrong (D): This reverses the analysis. The minor detour, not the personal frolic, falls within scope of employment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 67;

-- Q69: Business Structures [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["S corporations cannot have any shareholders and operate solely through appointed managers","C corporations are pass-through entities while S corporations face entity-level taxation","S corporation income passes through to shareholders and is taxed only once, avoiding double taxation","C corporations are restricted to a maximum of 100 shareholders under the IRC"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 1366, an S corporation''s income, losses, deductions, and credits pass through to shareholders'' individual returns. This avoids the double taxation that C corporations face under IRC Sections 11 and 301, where income is taxed at the entity level and again when distributed as dividends.
Wrong (A): S corporations must have shareholders. The 100-shareholder limit applies to S corporations, not a prohibition on shareholders.
Wrong (B): This reverses the treatment. S corporations are pass-through entities; C corporations face entity-level taxation.
Wrong (D): The 100-shareholder limit applies to S corporations under IRC Section 1361(b)(1)(A), not to C corporations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 69;

-- Q71: Property Transactions: Basis [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No difference exists because both gift and inheritance produce the same $55,000 gain on immediate sale at FMV","Inheritance produces $0 gain due to a stepped-up basis equal to FMV, while a gift produces $55,000 gain from carryover basis","Gift produces $0 gain because all gifts are excluded from taxation, while inheritance produces $55,000 gain","Both produce $0 gain because transfers between family members are always treated as nontaxable events"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1014, inherited property receives a stepped-up basis to FMV at the date of death ($95,000), so selling immediately at $95,000 produces $0 gain. Under Section 1015, gift property carries over the donor''s basis ($40,000), so selling at $95,000 produces a $55,000 gain. This $55,000 difference illustrates why the stepped-up basis at death is one of the most significant tax benefits in the Code.
Wrong (A): The two transfer methods produce fundamentally different basis outcomes, resulting in different gain amounts.
Wrong (C): While gifts are excluded from income to the recipient, the carryover basis preserves the donor''s built-in gain for later recognition upon sale.
Wrong (D): Tax treatment depends on the transfer method (gift vs. inheritance), not the family relationship between the parties.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 71;

-- Q73: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Hoffman has $15,000 ordinary income under Section 1245 (full recapture of gain up to depreciation taken); Klein has $50,000 taxed at the 25% unrecaptured Section 1250 rate for straight-line real property depreciation","Both have Section 1231 gains because any business asset held over one year qualifies for capital gain treatment regardless of depreciation","Hoffman has $15,000 long-term capital gain and Klein has $50,000 ordinary income, because real property faces harsher recapture than personal property","Both have ordinary income equal to total depreciation taken ($35,000 and $150,000 respectively), regardless of actual gain on the sale"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 1245 requires full recapture of depreciation as ordinary income to the extent of gain. Hoffman''s $15,000 gain ($80,000 minus $65,000 basis) is entirely within the $35,000 of depreciation taken, so all $15,000 is ordinary income. Section 1250 treats real property differently: Klein''s $50,000 gain attributable to straight-line depreciation is taxed as unrecaptured Section 1250 gain at a maximum 25% rate, not full ordinary income rates.
Wrong (B): Depreciation recapture under Sections 1245 and 1250 overrides Section 1231 treatment to the extent of prior depreciation.
Wrong (C): This reverses the relative harshness. Personal property (Section 1245) faces full ordinary recapture, which is harsher than the 25% rate for real property.
Wrong (D): Recapture is limited to actual gain realized, not total depreciation taken. Hoffman''s gain is only $15,000, not $35,000.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 73;

-- Q77: Individual Taxation: Income [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Brennan reports in 2025 because the check was received and available without substantial limitation; Sato reports in 2026 because the funds were genuinely restricted until January","Both report in 2026 because income is recognized only when cash is physically deposited, regardless of when it first becomes available","Both report in 2025 because the employer''s legal obligation to pay arose in 2025, which controls the timing for both employees","Brennan reports in 2026 when deposited while Sato reports in 2025 when promised, because constructive receipt applies to promises rather than possession"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 451 and Treasury Regulation Section 1.451-2, income is taxable when credited, set apart, or made available without substantial limitation. Brennan had unrestricted access to the $10,000 check on December 28, 2025, so income is recognized in 2025 despite depositing in January 2026. Sato faced a genuine restriction because the funds were unavailable until January 2026, so no constructive receipt occurred in 2025.
Wrong (B): Constructive receipt does not require physical deposit. Income is taxable when available without restriction, not when deposited.
Wrong (C): The employer''s obligation is irrelevant. The key is whether the taxpayer had unrestricted access to the funds in the tax year.
Wrong (D): This reverses the analysis. Brennan, who had possession, reports in 2025. A mere promise without access does not trigger constructive receipt.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 77;

-- Q80: Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["SALT deductions have no effect on AMT because state and local taxes are not treated as an AMT preference or adjustment item","The $35,000 SALT addback is the primary AMT driver, pushing TMT above regular tax; eliminating AMT would require reducing AMTI below the breakeven point through lower SALT or higher regular tax","The AMT equals $52,000 (the full TMT amount) regardless of regular tax liability, because the AMT calculation ignores the regular tax entirely","The couple can always avoid AMT by electing not to claim the SALT deduction, and this strategy invariably produces a better overall result"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 55, AMT is the excess of tentative minimum tax over regular tax liability. Under Section 56(b)(1)(A), the SALT deduction is added back in computing AMTI. The $35,000 SALT addback pushes TMT to $52,000, creating $7,000 in AMT. Eliminating AMT would require reducing AMTI below the point where TMT equals regular tax.
Wrong (A): SALT deductions are one of the largest AMT adjustment items under Section 56(b)(1)(A) and directly increase AMTI.
Wrong (C): AMT is the excess of TMT over regular tax ($52,000 minus $45,000 = $7,000), not the full TMT amount.
Wrong (D): Forgoing the SALT deduction increases regular tax, which may or may not produce a better result depending on the taxpayer''s specific circumstances.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 80;

-- Q85: S Corporations [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Having fewer than 50 shareholders, because S corporations require a minimum shareholder count","Issuing a second class of stock with different distribution or liquidation rights","Distributing profits to shareholders through ordinary dividend payments","Filing a consolidated return with affiliated corporations in the same group"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1361(b)(1)(D), an S corporation may have only one class of stock. Issuing preferred stock with different distribution or liquidation rights creates a second class and automatically terminates the S election under Section 1362(d)(2). Differences in voting rights alone do not create a second class under Treasury Regulation Section 1.1361-1(l)(1).
Wrong (A): There is no minimum shareholder count. The limit is a maximum of 100 shareholders under Section 1361(b)(1)(A).
Wrong (C): Distributing profits is a normal S corporation activity and does not affect the election, provided all distributions respect the single class of stock requirement.
Wrong (D): S corporations cannot file consolidated returns, but that restriction does not cause loss of the S election itself.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 85;

-- Q199: Circular 230 [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The fee is automatically unconscionable because it exceeds $5,000, which is the Circular 230 maximum for individual return preparation","Unconscionability uses a multi-factor test considering the practitioner''s skill and experience, complexity of the work, customary charges for similar services, and results obtained","Only the client''s ability to pay is relevant; if the client can afford the fee, it cannot be unconscionable regardless of the work actually performed","Only the practitioner''s office location and overhead costs matter, because Circular 230 requires fees to be proportional to operating expenses"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Circular 230 Section 10.27, a practitioner may not charge an unconscionable fee. The determination uses a multi-factor test evaluating the practitioner''s experience and reputation, novelty and difficulty of the questions, skill required, customary charges in the locality, and time limitations. A $25,000 fee for a simple W-2 return is likely unconscionable because complexity is minimal and the fee vastly exceeds customary charges.
Wrong (A): Circular 230 imposes no fixed dollar cap. Unconscionability is context-dependent, not threshold-based.
Wrong (C): Client ability to pay is not the standard. The analysis focuses on the nature of the services and customary charges.
Wrong (D): Overhead costs are not the controlling factor. The test examines multiple factors including complexity, skill, and prevailing market rates.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 199;

-- Q211: Business Structures [medium/L1]
