-- Q251: Professional Ethics in Tax [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The CPA personally agrees with the position, regardless of whether supporting legal authority exists","There is a realistic possibility of the position being sustained on its merits, generally at least a one-in-three likelihood","The position will definitely save the client money, which justifies taking any available return position","Another CPA has previously taken the same position on a different client''s return"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AICPA SSTS No. 1, a CPA should not recommend a tax return position unless there is a realistic possibility of being sustained on its merits, generally interpreted as at least a one-in-three (33%) likelihood. If the position does not meet this standard, it may still be taken if it has a reasonable basis and is adequately disclosed on the return.
Wrong (A): Personal agreement is insufficient. The standard requires objective support from legal authority, not subjective belief.
Wrong (C): Tax savings do not justify a position. The position must have a realistic possibility of being sustained regardless of financial benefit.
Wrong (D): Another CPA''s prior use of a position does not establish the required level of authority or likelihood of success.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 251;

-- Q252: Professional Ethics in Tax [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Amend the return without informing the client, to correct the error as quickly as possible","Do nothing since the return has already been filed and accepted by the IRS for processing","Report the client directly to the IRS, because CPAs are mandatory reporters of client tax errors","Promptly inform the client of the error, explain potential consequences, and recommend corrective action"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AICPA SSTS No. 6, when a CPA discovers an error on a previously filed return still open under the statute of limitations, the CPA must promptly inform the client and recommend corrective action such as filing an amended return. The decision to correct the error remains the client''s responsibility.
Wrong (A): The CPA cannot amend the return without the client''s knowledge and authorization. The client must be informed first.
Wrong (B): The CPA has an affirmative duty to notify the client of discovered errors, not to ignore them because the return was already filed.
Wrong (C): CPAs are not required to report clients to the IRS. The obligation is to advise the client, not to serve as an informant.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 252;

-- Q491: Circular 230 [easy/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both returns have identical audit risk because DIF scores are assigned randomly without regard to return characteristics or income levels","Briggs faces higher audit risk because W-2 wage income is scrutinized more closely than self-employment income under the DIF scoring model","Archer''s return likely generates a higher DIF score because the 91% expense ratio and home office deduction deviate sharply from norms for that income class","Neither return faces audit risk because individual returns under $200,000 in total income are exempt from DIF scoring entirely"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 6103, the IRS DIF system assigns scores based on how significantly a return''s characteristics deviate from statistical norms for comparable returns. Archer''s 91% expense-to-revenue ratio and home office deduction produce a high DIF score because they deviate substantially from expected profiles for self-employment returns at the $180,000 income level.
Wrong (A): DIF scores are not random. The system uses statistical models comparing returns to norms for similar income classes and filing types.
Wrong (B): W-2 returns with standard deductions closely match statistical norms, producing low DIF scores. Self-employment returns with high expense ratios trigger elevated scores.
Wrong (D): No income threshold exempts returns from DIF scoring. The system evaluates all returns regardless of dollar amounts.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 491;

-- Q501: Agency and Regulation [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The investor must prove scienter under both Section 11 and Rule 10b-5 because all securities fraud claims require proof of the defendant''s intent to deceive","Section 11 requires only a material misstatement and damages, with no need to prove scienter or reliance; Rule 10b-5 requires scienter, materiality, reliance, causation, and damages","Section 11 requires more elements than Rule 10b-5 because registration statement claims receive greater legal scrutiny than general fraud claims","Neither claim requires scienter because securities law imposes strict liability for all types of investor protection claims"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 11 imposes near-strict liability for misstatements in registration statements. The plaintiff needs only show a material misstatement or omission and damages, with no requirement to prove scienter, reliance, or causation. Rule 10b-5 requires the full set of fraud elements: materiality, scienter (intent to deceive or recklessness), reliance, economic loss, and loss causation. Scienter is the critical differentiator, transforming the claim from a documentation accuracy standard into a fraud standard.
Wrong (A): Section 11 does not require scienter. It was designed as a near-strict-liability provision to ensure registration statement accuracy.
Wrong (C): This reverses the relative burden. Section 11 requires fewer elements than Rule 10b-5, not more.
Wrong (D): Rule 10b-5 specifically requires scienter. Only Section 11 approaches strict liability, and even then, non-issuer defendants have due diligence defenses.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 501;

-- Q505: Business Structures [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The creditor extended the payment period without the surety''s consent, releasing the surety to the extent of resulting prejudice","The principal debtor currently has sufficient assets available to satisfy the debt in full","The surety was unaware of the exact amount of the underlying debt at the time the guarantee was made","The creditor failed to first demand payment from the principal debtor before pursuing the surety"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Restatement (Third) of Suretyship and Guaranty Section 37, a surety may be discharged if the creditor materially modifies the obligation without the surety''s consent, to the extent the surety is prejudiced. Extending the payment period alters the risk the surety originally assumed.
Wrong (B): The principal debtor''s solvency is not a defense for the surety. A surety is primarily liable and can be pursued regardless of the debtor''s assets.
Wrong (C): Lack of awareness of the debt amount is generally not a valid defense if the surety agreed to the guarantee.
Wrong (D): Unlike a guarantor of collection, a surety is primarily liable. The creditor need not exhaust remedies against the principal debtor first.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 505;

-- Q506: Business Structures [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["All four classes split the $800,000 pro rata because bankruptcy law treats all creditors and equity holders equally","Administrative expenses are paid first ($100,000), then employee wages ($150,000), then trade creditors receive $400,000 from the remaining $550,000, leaving $150,000 for equity holders","Equity holders are paid first because shareholders invested capital and deserve priority return of their investment over creditors","Employee wages receive first priority, then equity holders, then trade creditors, and finally administrative expenses are paid last"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 507 of the Bankruptcy Code establishes a strict priority hierarchy. Administrative expenses (Section 507(a)(2)) receive highest priority because the bankruptcy system depends on professionals willing to administer estates. Employee wages (Section 507(a)(4)) rank next, reflecting Congress''s policy to protect workers. General unsecured creditors follow, with equity holders last. Here, $800,000 covers all creditor classes with $150,000 remaining for equity holders.
Wrong (A): Bankruptcy uses strict priority, not pro rata distribution across all classes. Higher-priority claims must be paid in full before lower classes receive anything.
Wrong (C): Equity holders are the lowest priority. They accepted ownership risk and receive distributions only after all creditors are satisfied.
Wrong (D): This completely reverses the statutory priority order established by Section 507.$EXPL$
