-- Q512: Property Transactions: Basis [easy/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The van produces larger first-year deductions because 5-year property recovers cost faster than 7-year property, both using 200% declining balance under MACRS","No difference because all business personal property uses straight-line depreciation and produces identical annual deductions under MACRS","The furniture produces larger deductions because 7-year property uses a more accelerated method to compensate for the longer recovery period","Both assets produce identical deductions because MACRS assigns the same recovery period to all business personal property"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 168(e) and Rev. Proc. 87-56, vehicles are 5-year MACRS property while office furniture is 7-year property. Both use 200% declining balance under Sec. 168(b)(1). Under the half-year convention per Sec. 168(d)(1), 5-year property yields a 20% first-year rate versus 14.29% for 7-year property, so the van generates larger early deductions.
Wrong (B): Both asset classes use 200% declining balance (accelerated), not straight-line. Different recovery periods produce different annual deductions.
Wrong (C): The 7-year class does not use a more accelerated method. Both use the same 200% declining balance method, but the shorter recovery period produces faster cost recovery.
Wrong (D): MACRS assigns different recovery periods based on asset class. Vehicles and furniture are classified differently based on estimated useful life.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 512;

-- Q518: Property Transactions: Basis [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No meaningful difference because both methods produce the same total deduction over the asset''s life with only cosmetic labeling differences","Thornton claims larger early deductions because ADS uses accelerated rates over the longer 9-year recovery period","Thornton claims smaller annual deductions over 9 years using straight-line, while Waverly front-loads deductions over 5 years using 200% declining balance","Thornton deducts the full $40,000 immediately under ADS while Waverly must spread the cost over the 5-year GDS recovery period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 168(g), ADS requires straight-line depreciation over a longer recovery period (9 years here versus 5 years under GDS), producing smaller annual deductions. While total depreciation is identical ($40,000), the timing difference significantly affects cash flow. A company might elect ADS under Section 168(g)(7) to avoid creating unusable NOLs or to eliminate AMT depreciation adjustments.
Wrong (A): Although total depreciation is the same, the timing difference between the methods has real economic effects on cash flow and tax planning.
Wrong (B): ADS uses straight-line, not accelerated rates. It produces smaller, not larger, annual deductions compared to GDS.
Wrong (D): ADS does not allow immediate expensing. It requires straight-line depreciation spread over the longer ADS recovery period.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 518;

-- Q523: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Section 1231 alone would produce $50,000 of long-term capital gain; Section 1245 has no effect on equipment because it applies exclusively to real property","Section 1231 alone would produce $50,000 of potential capital gain; Section 1245 recaptures the full $50,000 as ordinary income because the gain does not exceed the $80,000 of depreciation taken","Section 1245 converts only $30,000 to ordinary income (gain above original cost) and leaves $20,000 as Section 1231 capital gain","Both Section 1231 and Section 1245 treat the full $50,000 as capital gain because gains on business property are always capital in nature"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Without Section 1245, the $50,000 gain would flow through Section 1231 and potentially qualify for capital gain rates. Section 1245 overrides this by recapturing gain as ordinary income to the extent of all prior depreciation. Since the $50,000 gain is less than the $80,000 of accumulated depreciation, the entire $50,000 is ordinary income. This prevents taxpayers from deducting depreciation at ordinary rates and recovering the amount at lower capital gain rates.
Wrong (A): Section 1245 applies to personal property (equipment), not real property. Section 1250 covers real property recapture.
Wrong (C): Section 1245 recaptures all depreciation to the extent of gain, not just gain above original cost. The $80,000 of depreciation exceeds the $50,000 gain.
Wrong (D): Section 1245 explicitly converts what would otherwise be capital gain into ordinary income up to the amount of depreciation taken.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 523;

