UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both gains are taxed at the same 20% rate because all long-term capital assets held over one year receive identical treatment","The coin collection gain is taxed at a maximum 28% rate while the stock gain is taxed at 20%, costing Whitfield an additional $3,200 on the same gain amount","The coin collection gain is fully exempt from tax because collectibles held over one year qualify for a complete capital gains exclusion","The stock gain is taxed at 28% and the collectible gain at 15%, because the Code penalizes financial asset gains more heavily"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 1(h), collectibles (coins, art, stamps, gems, antiques) held more than one year are taxed at a maximum 28% capital gains rate, versus 15%/20% for most other long-term capital gains. For Whitfield in the 35% bracket, the stock gain is taxed at 20% ($8,000) while the collectible gain is taxed at 28% ($11,200), a $3,200 differential on identical $40,000 gains.
Wrong (A): The Code explicitly creates a separate, higher rate for collectibles, not a uniform rate for all long-term capital gains.
Wrong (C): No complete exclusion exists for collectibles. They receive long-term capital gain treatment but at a higher maximum rate.
Wrong (D): This reverses the rate relationship. Collectibles face the higher 28% rate, while financial assets such as stock receive the lower 15%/20% rate.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 525;

-- Q532: Like-Kind Exchanges [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reports the gain on Schedule D in the year of the involuntary conversion event","Reinvests the proceeds in similar-use replacement property within the statutory replacement period","Donates the full amount of insurance proceeds to a qualified charitable organization","Elects installment sale treatment to spread the recognized gain over multiple tax years"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 1033, gain from an involuntary conversion may be deferred if the taxpayer purchases qualified replacement property within the replacement period. The replacement property must be similar or related in service or use (or like-kind for condemned real property). The replacement period is generally two years after the close of the tax year of conversion.
Wrong (A): Reporting gain on Schedule D in the conversion year is the default treatment, not a method for deferring the gain.
Wrong (C): Donating insurance proceeds to charity does not satisfy Section 1033. Deferral requires reinvestment in replacement property.
Wrong (D): Installment sale treatment under Section 453 is a separate provision and does not apply to involuntary conversion deferral under Section 1033.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 532;

-- Q533: Like-Kind Exchanges [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Henderson recognizes $20,000 gain (proceeds minus reinvestment) because only the unreinvested portion is taxable; Diaz recognizes $0 because reinvestment exceeds proceeds, deferring the full $150,000 gain into a lower replacement basis","Henderson recognizes $150,000 and Diaz recognizes $0, because any shortfall in reinvestment triggers recognition of the entire realized gain","Both recognize $0 because Section 1033 defers all gain whenever the taxpayer acquires any replacement property regardless of reinvestment amount","Henderson recognizes $0 and Diaz recognizes $20,000, because reinvesting more than the proceeds creates a taxable event on the excess"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 1033, gain is recognized only to the extent proceeds exceed replacement property cost. Henderson: $500,000 minus $480,000 = $20,000 recognized; $130,000 deferred into a $350,000 replacement basis. Diaz: $520,000 reinvestment exceeds $500,000 proceeds, so $0 is recognized and the full $150,000 is deferred into a $370,000 basis. The policy rationale is that involuntary conversions are compelled events, so deferral is appropriate when proceeds are fully reinvested.
Wrong (B): Gain recognition is limited to the shortfall amount, not the full realized gain. Henderson recognizes only $20,000, not $150,000.
Wrong (C): Complete deferral requires reinvestment at or above the proceeds amount. Partial reinvestment triggers partial recognition.
Wrong (D): This reverses the treatment. Excess reinvestment produces no gain; it is shortfalls that trigger recognition.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 533;

-- Q537: Like-Kind Exchanges [easy/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both sales qualify for installment method treatment because Section 453 applies to any sale with deferred payments regardless of asset type","The stock sale is excluded because publicly traded securities have readily ascertainable market value, making deferral unjustified; the rental property qualifies because real estate lacks a liquid public market","The stock sale qualifies for installment treatment but the rental property does not, because real estate is excluded from all gain deferral provisions","Neither sale qualifies because the TCJA repealed the installment method for all asset types effective 2018"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 453(k)(2) excludes publicly traded securities from installment method treatment because they have readily ascertainable fair market value. A taxpayer selling stock on installment terms could just as easily sell for immediate cash, so deferral is unjustified. Rental property lacks a liquid public market and often requires seller financing, making installment deferral a legitimate accommodation.
Wrong (A): Section 453(k) explicitly excludes certain asset types including publicly traded securities, so the method does not apply universally.
Wrong (C): This reverses the treatment. Real estate is one of the most common installment method transactions; publicly traded securities are excluded.
Wrong (D): The installment method was not repealed by the TCJA. It remains available for qualifying sales of non-publicly-traded property.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 537;

-- Q543: Individual Taxation: Income [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No change because Social Security benefits are always fully excluded from gross income regardless of other income sources or provisional income level","In 2025, 50% of benefits ($12,000) are includible because provisional income is between $25,000 and $34,000; in 2026, up to 85% ($20,400) are includible because IRA withdrawals pushed provisional income above $34,000","In both years 100% of benefits are includible because any provisional income above $25,000 triggers full inclusion of all Social Security benefits received","In 2025, 85% is includible and in 2026, 50% is includible, because higher provisional income reduces the taxable percentage as progressive relief"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 86(a) and (b), Social Security benefits are included based on two provisional income thresholds for single filers: $25,000 and $34,000. In 2025, provisional income of $30,000 falls between thresholds, triggering up to 50% inclusion under Sec. 86(a)(1). In 2026, IRA withdrawals push provisional income to $48,000, exceeding $34,000 and triggering up to 85% inclusion under Sec. 86(a)(2).
Wrong (A): Social Security benefits are not always excluded. Inclusion depends on the taxpayer''s provisional income relative to the statutory thresholds.
Wrong (C): The 100% inclusion rate does not exist. The maximum includible percentage under Section 86 is 85%, not 100%.
Wrong (D): This reverses the tiers. Higher provisional income increases (not decreases) the taxable percentage of Social Security benefits.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 543;

-- Q558: Individual Taxation: Credits [easy/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both credits are available and Nguyen should simply claim whichever credit produces the larger dollar benefit on the return","Neither credit is available because Nguyen''s AGI of $65,000 exceeds the income phase-out threshold for both education credits","The AOTC is available but the LLC is not, because the AOTC covers graduate study while the LLC applies only to undergraduates","The LLC is available because it covers graduate programs with no enrollment intensity limit; the AOTC is unavailable because it requires first four years and at least half-time enrollment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): The AOTC under Section 25A(b) is restricted to the first four years of postsecondary education, requires at least half-time enrollment, and applies only to degree-seeking students. Nguyen''s graduate certificate program fails multiple AOTC requirements. The LLC under Section 25A(c) covers any postsecondary coursework with no enrollment intensity or year limits. Nguyen''s $8,500 generates a $1,700 LLC (20% of $8,500), and her $65,000 AGI is below the phase-out.
Wrong (A): The AOTC is not available to Nguyen due to eligibility restrictions on graduate and non-degree programs.
Wrong (B): The $65,000 AGI is below the phase-out threshold for both credits. The LLC phases out above $80,000 for single filers.
Wrong (C): This reverses the credit restrictions. The AOTC is limited to undergraduates in the first four years; the LLC covers graduate study.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 558;

-- Q578: S Corporations [easy/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reclassification has no tax effect because S corporation distributions and salary are taxed identically for employment tax purposes","Reclassification subjects $120,000 to FICA taxes (Social Security at 6.2% each for employer and employee plus Medicare at 1.45% each), creating approximately $18,360 in combined employee FICA liability","Reclassification converts the $120,000 from ordinary income to capital gains, reducing Crawford''s overall effective tax rate on that amount","The IRS cannot reclassify distributions because S corporation shareholders have absolute discretion over the salary-versus-distribution allocation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 3121(a), wages are subject to FICA, while S corporation distributions under Section 1368 are not. When Crawford takes $200,000 entirely as distributions with zero salary, no FICA taxes are paid. Reclassification of $120,000 as reasonable compensation triggers Social Security tax (6.2% each) and Medicare tax (1.45% each), creating significant employment tax liability the IRS targets this arrangement to prevent.
Wrong (A): Distributions and salary are not taxed identically. Salary triggers FICA taxes while distributions do not, which is precisely why the IRS reclassifies.
Wrong (C): Reclassification converts distributions to wages (ordinary income subject to FICA), not to capital gains. It increases, not decreases, the tax burden.
Wrong (D): The IRS can and does reclassify distributions as reasonable compensation when shareholder-employees perform services but take no salary.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 578;
